# roof — the lead flats behind the parapet: the lid on the whole building.
#
# One lead deck over the whole main block, laid in four level bays that step down away from the drum
# to a parapet gutter running the entire perimeter, with rolls down the fall and an outlet gully in
# the middle of each of the four sides. It lives between 11.85 and 12.15, and 12.15 is where a
# balustrade of the order's own proportions tops its plinth out, so none of it is ever seen from the
# ground and all of it is seen from the air.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD: it is the LID, and the lid is what makes an interior an interior.
#
# The rotunda, the vestibule, the halls, every coffer and niche in them, are lit by what comes down
# the oculus and through sixteen drum windows of 1.05 m² apiece. That is the hardest indirect-light
# problem in the building and a large part of why the building exists. ALL OF IT IS MEANINGLESS IF
# THIS PART LEAKS. One hole in the roof and the interior is lit by a skylight nobody drew: the
# bounce terms stop being bounce terms, the occlusion under a coffer fills in, the drum's splayed
# reveals stop being the brightest surface in the room, and every measurement taken inside is a
# measurement of the leak. A roof that is 99% closed is 0% useful.
#
# So the deck is SOLID — not a skin, not a shell — and it is continuous over the whole 30.20 x 13.20
# opening the walls leave. It is lapped into both its neighbours rather than butted against them,
# because the manifest displaces the finished building by 12 mm of grain at the very end and a joint
# that only touches can be opened by it:
#
#   at the wall   the deck runs out to +-15.85 and +-7.35 — 0.75 past the wall's inner face at
#                 +-15.10 and +-6.60, and 0.15 short of the block outline so it can never show on an
#                 elevation — and its soffit is at 11.85, which is 0.05 below the wall head at 11.90.
#                 It also laps 0.03 under parapet.clip's plinth, which stands on +-15.82 / +-7.32.
#   at the drum   the deck's inner face is a cylinder of radius 5.80 and drum.clip's plinth is an
#                 annulus from 5.30 to 5.85 standing to 12.15. They share 0.05 of radius over the
#                 deck's whole depth, and the plinth's top is exactly the head of the fall, so the
#                 stone and the lead make one continuous surface at 12.15 with no ledge in it.
#
# THE SECOND LOAD — the only large brushed metal in the scene, and it already found something.
#
# `lead` (rgb 118,120,124 rough=170 metal=140 brush=2) is used by nothing else in this building.
# There are about 340 m² of it here, all of it level, which is the one place a brushed-metal lobe
# either shows or does not: a rough anisotropic metal lit by the sun at a glancing angle and by the
# whole sky everywhere else. Every other metal in this scene is small (a door, a swag, a rosette, a
# glazing bar) or vertical or both. This is the sheet, and it is what a sheet is for.
#
# THE ANSWER IS THAT IT DOES NOT SHOW: `brush` COMES BACK AS GLITTER. Path-traced, this deck reads as
# gravel or old snow — fixed white speckle two or three voxels across, over the whole 340 m². It is
# not the manifest's `variation` (identical with the line and without it), it is not my paint (making
# the base coat porphyry changed nothing, so every voxel really is lead), and it is not Monte Carlo
# noise (identical at 72 frames and at 600). It is `brush`. A twelve-line clip with three bands of
# the same colour and roughness — one `stone`, one `metal=140`, one `metal=140 brush=2` — comes back
# with the first two smooth and only the brushed one speckled, from every angle, at any sample count.
# Repro clip and images in clips/facility/requests/roof.md.
#
# I have left the material alone. `lead` is the contract's and the contract is not mine to edit, this
# roof is the biggest thing in the building wearing it, and a finding this size is worth more than a
# tidy render. At building scale and at ordinary angles it still reads as a grey lead roof; it is
# close raking views that show the speckle. If the shading is not the thing that gets fixed, the
# fallback is one line in the contract — a `lead_sheet` without `brush` — and that is a request, not
# something a fragment should do for itself.
#
# THE THIRD LOAD — thin matter, three ways, on that metal.
#
#   the rolls    26 lead rolls, 0.09 m across (M/5) standing 0.045 m proud (M/10). They run down the
#                fall and the drum cuts them off, so they are 8.55 m long on the centre line and
#                12.2 m at the outside — 250 m of them. That is 1.4 voxels of relief at the
#                contract's metre 32, on metal, with no geometry under it to help a denoiser guess
#                where the line goes. A roll is either a bright line with a dark one beside it or it
#                is nothing.
#   the drips    six drip lines, 0.0375 m high (M/12) and 11.70 m long: 70 m of a step ONE AND A
#                FIFTH VOXELS tall, across the fall, on level lead. A step that shallow is the
#                smallest honest piece of geometry in this building — smaller than a coffer's
#                moulding step, and unlike a coffer's it is out in the sun. A welted NOSING along
#                each of them was built, measured and taken out again; see THE NOSINGS THAT ARE
#                NOT HERE, below, for the four components it cost and how that was localised.
#   the gullies  four outlet gratings, 20 slots between them, each 0.045 m wide (M/10) with 0.045 m
#                of bar either side, cut through a 0.045 m plate into a sump. Bar and slot are the
#                same width and both are one and a half voxels, at the bottom of a channel that is
#                in the parapet's shadow all day.
#
# DO NOT THICKEN ANY OF THEM. 0.0375, 0.045 and 0.09 are M/12, M/10 and M/5 — the last two are the
# numbers the entablature's dentils are built on — and they are chosen to be one to one and a half
# voxels because that is the width at which a sampler either keeps a feature or smears it.
#
# NO SLATE, though the brief allows it, and NO COPPER. This roof falls 0.15 m in 8.55 — 1 in 57 —
# because 0.15 m is all the height there is between drum.clip's plinth at 12.15 and a gutter that
# still has to have 0.15 m of deck under it. Slate needs 1 in 3. Lead is what is laid at 1 in 57 and
# lead is what is here, in bays between rolls, the way lead is actually laid. Copper is kept off it
# for the reason a roofer would give: copper and lead in the same gutter eat each other. The one
# piece of copper that touches this roof is drum.clip's own apron, and that is theirs, and it is a
# flashing.
#
# NO WEATHERING, and this one is worth writing down because it was tried and measured. A single
# `weather overgrown 0.10 on=<the gutter>` — moss in the channel, which is exactly what weathering
# is for and exactly where it belongs — took this part from 110 ms to 17,646 ms at metre 8. A
# hundred and sixty times, for one coat on one channel. The scoping works and it is not the scope
# that costs: it is `occlusion`, `curvature` and `facing` being asked of the whole clip once per
# solid voxel. Measured both ways and written up in clips/facility/requests/roof.md.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT COMES OFF THE SLAB, measured by building the part with each cut and again without it, at the
# contract's metre 32:
#
#   roof_well        33.03 m3   the drum's ground, a cylinder of 5.80 cut clean through
#   roof_cut_gutter  15.71 m3   the channel, 1.20 wide and 0.15 deep, round all four sides
#   roof_drips       10.94 m3   the three steps that take the fall
#   roof_sumps        0.062 m3  four outlet sumps, 0.45 square and 0.09 below the sole
#   the well, again   0.733 m3  taken out of the ROLLS, so each starts where the drum's circle is
#   the grating slots 0.011 m3  twenty slots, 0.045 wide through a 0.045 plate. At metre 8 this cut
#                               removes EXACTLY NOTHING — 0.045 is 0.36 of a voxel there — and it
#                               is a genuine cut all the same. A slot audited at the wrong
#                               resolution reads as a knife that missed.
#
# This part removes nothing from anybody else and the manifest names no `void_roof`.
#
# 85.065 m3 of lead in ONE component with NOTHING floating, at metre 32. That is the number this
# part is judged by and it was 5 components and 288 floating voxels until the drips were given
# their lap — see THE DRIPS below.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THE FALL IS TAKEN IN FOUR STEPS AND NOT AS A SLOPE. This is the thing worth reading in this
# file, and it was found on a contact sheet.
#
# The first version was one plane tilted 1 in 57 from the drum to the gutter, which is what a roof
# is. A voxel at the contract's metre 32 is 0.03125 m, so that plane loses a whole voxel of height
# every 1.78 m and the sampler builds it as a flight of 1.78 m treads — it has no choice, and no
# sampler ever will. On its own that is fine and nobody would look twice. What it does to the ROLLS
# is not fine: a roll stands 0.045 m proud, one and two fifths of a voxel, so on the half of every
# tread where the terrace has already rounded the surface up, the roll has nothing left to stand on
# and disappears. Rendered at metre 32 the rolls came back as DASHES — a metre on, a metre off, all
# the way down every one of the twenty-six. Not noise, not the denoiser: arithmetic.
#
# A slope shallower than one voxel per voxel is not a slope. It is a staircase the engine chose for
# you, with the tread length picked by the arithmetic of the pitch. So this roof takes the fall in a
# staircase IT chose: four level bays, each stepping 0.0375 (M/12) down to the next at a drip, which
# is exactly how lead is laid over a long fall in the real trade and for a related reason — lead
# sheet cannot run more than a couple of metres down a fall without creeping.
#
# Every surface on this roof is now exactly level, so every roll sits at one height for its whole
# length and reads as one unbroken line; and the six drips are a deliberate, crisp, 1.2-voxel step
# instead of an accidental, ragged, 1.0-voxel one. Same fall, same 0.15 m, same 1 in 57 over the
# whole run. If you are ever tempted to smooth this back into a plane, render the plan at metre 32
# first and count the dashes.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45. EVERY dimension below is a whole multiple or a simple fraction of
# one of them; this part has no remainders anywhere. The numbers that come from somewhere else:
#
#   16.00, 7.50   the contract's block outline, which the contract itself says is not on the module.
#   15.10, 6.60   the inner face of the wall — those less 0.90.
#   5.85          the face of drum.clip's plinth. It is 13 M, and it is also exactly 7.05 - 1.20:
#                 see THE PLAN below, because the whole layout hangs off that coincidence.
#
# THE LEVELS, all off the contract's entab_top = 11.90:
#
#   11.85     the soffit, everywhere            11.90 - 0.05: bedded into the wall head, and NOT ONE
#                                               MILLIMETRE LOWER — see the note under this table
#   11.90     entab_top, the wall head, and     the one line with nothing of mine on it, which is
#             the line the paint is split on    why the paint is split there — see THE SURFACE
#   11.91     the floor of an outlet sump       12.00 - 0.09 (M/5), and it sits on the wall head
#   11.955    the underside of a grating        12.00 - 0.045 (M/10)
#   12.00     the gutter sole                   11.85 + 0.15 (M/3), leaving M/3 of deck under it
#   12.0375   bay four                          each bay is M/12 above the one outside it
#   12.075    bay three
#   12.1125   bay two
#   12.15     bay one, at the drum, and the     27 M exactly. It is drum.clip's plinth top; it is
#             top of the gutter's back          also 11.90 + 0.25, where parapet.clip's plinth die
#                                               stops (11.85 - 12.1375) before its cyma sets back.
#   13.00     parapet_top. NOTHING HERE COMES WITHIN 0.85 OF IT.
#
# WHY THE SOFFIT NEVER GOES BELOW 11.85. A thicker deck would make a better roof — 0.30 of fall
# instead of 0.15 — and it would cost nothing but hanging the ceiling 0.20 lower, which is what a
# cornice does anyway. It is not done, and the reason is the load at the top of this file. The
# manifest subtracts void_vestibule, void_rotunda, void_halls and void_stair from the whole building
# AFTER this part is assembled, and every one of those fragments is entitled to carve its ceiling up
# to the wall head at 11.90. At a soffit of 11.85 the worst such cut takes 0.05 off the bottom of
# this deck and leaves 0.10 of solid stone under the gutter. At a soffit of 11.70 the same cut takes
# 0.20 and leaves nothing. A roof that is only light-tight until somebody else builds a room is not
# light-tight.
#
# THE PLAN, from the middle out, as half widths. Symmetric about both axes.
#
#   0.00 .. 5.80    THE DRUM. No roof: drum.clip's plinth is an annulus 5.30 .. 5.85 standing from
#                   11.25 to 12.15, so it closes this ground itself and laps this deck by 0.05.
#   5.80 .. 8.05    bay one,   2.25 (5 M), level 12.15
#   8.05 .. 10.30   bay two,   2.25 (5 M), level 12.1125
#   10.30 .. 12.55  bay three, 2.25 (5 M), level 12.075
#   12.55 .. 14.35  bay four,  1.80 (4 M), level 12.0375 — shorter, because it is the one that
#                   discharges into the channel and a short last bay is what a roofer leaves
#   14.35 .. 15.55  THE GUTTER, 1.20 wide (8M/3), sole 12.00, with a gully in the middle of each of
#                   the four sides. Its outer 0.45 is over the wall head.
#   15.55 .. 15.85  the gutter's back, top 12.15, standing on the wall head and lapped under the
#                   parapet's plinth.
#
# and in z the same, except that there is no room for any of the bays: 5.85 .. 7.05 is all gutter,
# and the deck runs level at 12.15 straight into it. 2.25 + 2.25 + 2.25 + 1.80 = 8.55 = 19 M.
#
# WHERE 1.20 COMES FROM, because everything else follows it. North and south of the drum the roof is
# the strip between drum.clip's plinth at 5.85 and the wall, and it must all be channel. A gutter
# has to get its outlet onto something solid, and the only solid thing up here is the wall head, so
# the channel is carried 0.45 (M) past the wall's inner face at 6.60, to 7.05. That makes it
# 7.05 - 5.85 = 1.20 wide (8M/3); the same width carried round the east and west puts its inner edge
# on 15.55 - 1.20 = 14.35; and 14.35 - 5.80 = 8.55, which is 19 M on the nose. There is no remainder
# in this file and there was no arithmetic done to make it so.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT drum.clip MAY WANT TO KNOW, AND IT IS ONE THING.
#
# Its comment says its foot stops at 11.25 so it is "buried in whatever the roof turns out to be",
# and it puts a copper apron at 11.70 .. 12.15 to dress the joint. The roof turns out to land at
# 12.15 — the top of its own plinth — and it cannot land lower: the gutter it drains to must have
# deck under it, that deck sits on a wall head at 11.90, and 12.00 is as low as a gutter sole can
# be. So the plinth is buried to its top and the apron is buried to its top, and what is left of the
# apron is a 0.075 m ring of copper lying flush in the lead at the drum's foot — which is exactly
# what an apron flashing is, and it does read: it comes out as a green ring round the well on the
# plan. Its socle stands 0.15 clear above this roof and its congé 0.30, both untouched. That is a
# good joint and nothing needs changing.
#
# But if drum.clip wants its copper to READ as a skirt rather than as a line, move the apron up to
# 12.15 .. 12.60 and it will stand on this roof instead of under it. Nothing else in either file
# moves. Written up in clips/facility/requests/roof.md.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE DECK — one solid slab, cut four ways
# ================================================================================================
#
# Everything here is a subtraction from one box, because a roof is defined by what comes off the top
# of it, and because a big box is the cheapest thing a sampler ever settles. The slab is drawn to
# its highest level, 12.15, and the drum's well, the gutter, the three inner drips and the four
# gullies are cut out of it.
#
# It reaches +-15.85 and +-7.35: 0.15 (M/3) short of the block outline, so that even with no parapet
# at all it could not appear on an elevation, and far enough out that it lands under any parapet
# that stands on this wall. It tops out at 12.15, which is where a balustrade of these proportions
# tops its plinth out, so no baluster can ever stand in it.
let roof_slab = box -15.85 11.85 -7.35   15.85 12.15 7.35  round=0.02

# THE WELL. A cylinder of 5.80, cut clean through. drum.clip's plinth runs out to 5.85 from 11.25 to
# 12.15, so the two share 0.05 of radius over the deck's whole depth, and the plinth's own top at
# 12.15 is flush with the head of the fall: the roof and the drum make one continuous surface at
# that level, with no ledge, no groove, and nothing for water or light to find.
let roof_well = cylinder 0 12.30 0  r=5.80 h=1.60 axis=y

# The rectangle everything inboard of the gutter is protected by. Its z half width is drum.clip's
# own 5.85, which is what makes the gutter one width the whole way round.
let roof_keep_field = box -14.35 11.95 -5.85   14.35 13.10 5.85  round=0.012

# THE GUTTER — the channel, 1.20 wide (8M/3), cut 0.15 (M/3) down from the slab's top, from the
# field's edge to 0.45 past the wall's inner face. A frame, so it turns all four corners with no
# corner geometry and no mitre to get wrong.
# Both boxes carry a round, and that is not decoration either: the cutter's arrises are the
# finished work's arrises turned inside out, so rounding the outer box puts a fillet along the top
# of the gutter's back and rounding the inner one chamfers the verge the lead turns down over. The
# brief asks for a small round on every edge; on a subtraction you get it by rounding the knife.
let roof_cut_gutter = difference {
    box -15.55 12.00 -7.05   15.55 13.10 7.05  round=0.012
    roof_keep_field
}

# THE DRIPS. Three boxes, each taking one more M/12 off everything outside its own drip line, which
# leaves four level bays stepping down away from the drum. Read the note at the top of this file
# before replacing them with a slope: they are the fall, and they are steps on purpose.
#
# Drawn once on +x and folded, which is what keeps the two halves of the building from ever
# drifting: `mirror` folds x to its absolute value, so the west bays are the east bays by
# construction rather than by a second set of numbers.
#
# THEY LAP 0.10 INTO THE GUTTER AND THEY USED TO BUTT AGAINST IT, AND THAT LEFT FOUR RODS OF LEAD
# HANGING IN THE AIR. This is the file's own rule about laps applied to a knife instead of to a
# solid, and it took a component count to find.
#
# Each drip was confined to |z| <= 5.85 and x <= 14.35 — exactly the faces of `roof_keep_field`,
# because north and south of 5.85 and outboard of 14.35 the gutter has already taken the top down
# to 12.00 and there was said to be nothing left to cut. The two knives therefore met on a shared
# plane, and where two knives meet on a shared plane the arithmetic decides who gets the voxel: at
# metre 32 the voxel column straddling z = +-5.85 over bay three came back solid at 12.06 while
# everything under it had been taken to the gutter sole, which left a rod of lead one voxel square
# and 2.25 m long floating over the gutter on each flank, four in all, 288 voxels. `components`
# said 5 for a part whose entire claim is that it is one piece.
#
# So the drips now run 0.10 past both faces, into ground the gutter has already cut to 12.00. That
# removes not one extra voxel — a cut to 12.0375 inside a hole that is already 12.00 is nothing —
# and it means neither knife ends where the other begins. 14.45 is still 1.10 clear of the gutter's
# back at 15.55, which must stay at 12.15, and 5.95 is 1.10 clear of the channel's outer edge at
# 7.05.
let roof_drip_two = box 8.05  12.1125 -5.95   14.45 13.10 5.95  round=0.012
let roof_drip_thr = box 10.30 12.0750 -5.95   14.45 13.10 5.95  round=0.012
let roof_drip_fou = box 12.55 12.0375 -5.95   14.45 13.10 5.95  round=0.012
let roof_drips = mirror { union { roof_drip_two roof_drip_thr roof_drip_fou } } axis=x

# THE GULLIES — one in the middle of each of the four sides, where a downpipe belongs. 0.45 square
# (M), sunk 0.09 (M/5) below the sole.
#
# THEY SIT IN THE OUTER HALF OF THE CHANNEL, OVER THE WALL HEAD, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THE
# CHANNEL IS 1.20 WIDE. A sump sunk into the inboard half would leave 0.06 m of deck under it,
# spanning the room; sunk here it has ten metres of masonry under it and a floor that cannot be
# thinned by anybody's ceiling. An outlet is the one place a gutter is weakest and it belongs over a
# wall.
let roof_sump_e = box 15.10 11.91 -0.225   15.55 13.10 0.225  round=0.02
let roof_sump_n = box -0.225 11.91 6.60    0.225 13.10 7.05   round=0.02
let roof_sumps = union {
    mirror { roof_sump_e } axis=x
    mirror { roof_sump_n } axis=z
}

let roof_deck = difference {
    roof_slab
    roof_well roof_cut_gutter roof_drips roof_sumps
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE ROLLS — 26 of them, 0.09 across, standing 0.045 proud, down the fall
# ================================================================================================
#
# Lead comes in sheets and the joint between two sheets running down a fall is a roll: the sheet is
# dressed over a timber batten and welted, which leaves a half round ridge. So it is a half round
# ridge — a capsule of radius 0.045 (M/10) whose AXIS LIES IN THE ROOF SURFACE, so exactly half of
# it stands proud and the other half is inside the deck and can never come adrift.
#
# One capsule to a bay, at that bay's own level, which is the whole reason the bays are level: a
# roll that sits at one height for its whole length is one unbroken line, and a roll on a slope is
# not. Each capsule starts 0.05 back inside the bay above it, so consecutive pieces overlap through
# the drip and the roll steps down with the lead, which is what a roll does at a drip.
#
# NOTE FOR ANYONE TIDYING THIS: these were once ONE capsule turned by 0.002792 to lie on a sloping
# plane, and that cost the building its sampler. `rotate` is deliberately given no bounding box
# (field.cpp: a bound that is wrong by a little produces a clip with pieces missing), and `repeat`
# declares INFINITE SLACK the moment its child has no finite box, because it cannot then know that a
# copy fits inside its own cell. Infinite slack anywhere means no box of the bounds can be settled
# ANYWHERE, so this bank of thirteen tubes went from 11,374 field evaluations to 91,501,146. Written
# out as axis-aligned capsules it is exact, bounded, and eight thousand times cheaper. If you ever
# do need a turned shape inside a repeat, intersect it with a box first — that is what gives it a
# box back. Written up in clips/facility/requests/roof.md.
#
# Then the drum is subtracted from the whole bank, so each roll starts where the drum's circle
# actually is: the one on the centre line begins at 5.80 and the outermost begins at 2.12, and their
# ends together trace the curve of the drum. That is what a roofer's setting out would do, and it
# costs one `difference`.
#
# 0.90 (D) between rolls, which is a bay of lead a roofer would actually lay. `repeat` is safe here
# where a polar repeat would not be — 0.09 of shape inside a 0.90 period fits in its own cell, so
# the field returns a true distance and the sampler can still settle boxes — and the seed sits at
# z = 0 for the same reason. See the note in _order.clip about what a polar repeat costs.
let roof_roll_one = union {
    capsule 0     12.1500 0    8.05  12.1500 0   r=0.045
    capsule 8.00  12.1125 0   10.30  12.1125 0   r=0.045
    capsule 10.25 12.0750 0   12.55  12.0750 0   r=0.045
    capsule 12.50 12.0375 0   14.35  12.0375 0   r=0.045
}
let roof_roll_bank = repeat { roof_roll_one } z=0.90 nz=6
let roof_rolls_east = difference { roof_roll_bank roof_well }
let roof_rolls = mirror { roof_rolls_east } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE NOSINGS THAT ARE NOT HERE — a welt at the head of each drip, tried, measured, and removed
# ================================================================================================
#
# A drip in lead is not a step in a plate. The upper sheet is dressed OVER the step and welted back
# under itself, and what that leaves along the top arris is a half round exactly like a roll turned
# through ninety degrees. Without it the three drips are the one place on this roof where the metal
# reads as a folded sheet of tin. So six capsules were built along the drip lines, at the level of
# the bay above each, of the rolls' own 0.045 radius — 70 m of welt lying across the 250 m of roll
# that runs the other way, which is what a lead flat actually is.
#
# THEY ARE NOT IN `part_roof`, AND THE REASON IS A NUMBER. With them the whole building at metre 8
# goes from 776 components to 780 and from 6111 floating voxels to 6171: four new islands of about
# fifteen voxels each. Not a lot, and under a parapet where nobody standing on the ground can see
# it, but the rule here is that a component count does not rise.
#
# It was localised rather than guessed, and it is worth writing down what the control arms said:
#
#   whole building, my five files reverted            776 components, 6111 floating
#   ... with dome.clip reverted only                  780               6171   — not the dome
#   ... with parapet and pilasters reverted           780               6171   — not those either
#   ... with roof.clip reverted only                  776               6111   — it is this file
#   ... with the drip lap kept, nosings dropped       776               6111   — it is the NOSINGS
#                                                                                and NOT the fix
#   nosings at r = 0.0375, 0.045 and 0.0675           780 every time — not a size effect
#   this part alone at metre 8, with and without      9 components either way
#   this part alone at metre 32, with them            1 component, nothing floating
#
# So it is not the geometry and it is not the resolution of this part: it is what the manifest does
# to the finished building afterwards. The deck's top at metre 8 lands one voxel layer below any
# feature that stands 0.0375 proud of it, so a welt lives alone in a layer the deck does not reach,
# and the manifest's 12 mm of grain is enough to part it from what is underneath. Any raised
# detail on this deck under 0.125 m will do the same thing; the rolls already do it and their
# islands are inside the 6111.
#
# TO PUT THEM BACK is one line — add `roof_noses` to `part_roof` below — and the cost is four
# islands at metre 8 and nothing at all at metre 32. That is a judgement about which resolution the
# count is being read at, and it is not this file's to make.
let roof_nose_two = capsule 8.05  12.1500 -5.85   8.05  12.1500 5.85  r=0.045
let roof_nose_thr = capsule 10.30 12.1125 -5.85   10.30 12.1125 5.85  r=0.045
let roof_nose_fou = capsule 12.55 12.0750 -5.85   12.55 12.0750 5.85  r=0.045
let roof_noses = mirror { union { roof_nose_two roof_nose_thr roof_nose_fou } } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE GRATINGS — a slotted plate over each gully
# ================================================================================================
#
# Five slots 0.045 wide (M/10) on a 0.09 pitch (M/5), cut through a plate 0.045 thick that is flush
# with the gutter sole and bedded 0.05 into it on all four sides. The bars between the slots are
# 0.045 across — the same as the slots, and one and a half voxels at the contract's metre 32.
#
# It is one plate with holes in it and not a bundle of loose bars, and that is deliberate: a bar
# 1.4 voxels thick spanning 0.35 m of nothing is a component waiting to fall off at any metre below
# 32, and this part's whole claim is that it is one piece with no holes in it. Held by its own rim,
# the thin matter is as thin as ever and cannot come adrift.
let roof_grate_plate_e = box 15.05 11.955 -0.275   15.60 12.00 0.275  round=0.012
let roof_grate_slot_e = box 15.15 11.90 -0.0225   15.50 12.05 0.0225
let roof_grate_e = difference {
    roof_grate_plate_e
    repeat { roof_grate_slot_e } z=0.09 nz=2
}

let roof_grate_plate_n = box -0.275 11.955 6.55   0.275 12.00 7.10  round=0.012
let roof_grate_slot_n = box -0.0225 11.90 6.65   0.0225 12.05 7.00
let roof_grate_n = difference {
    roof_grate_plate_n
    repeat { roof_grate_slot_n } x=0.09 nx=2
}

let roof_grates = union {
    mirror { roof_grate_e } axis=x
    mirror { roof_grate_n } axis=z
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let part_roof = union { roof_deck roof_rolls roof_grates }

# This part removes nothing from anybody else. The well, the gutter and the gullies are all cut out
# of my own slab, so the manifest never has to know they exist and there is no `void_roof`.

# ================================================================================================
# THE SURFACE
# ================================================================================================
#
# Two zones, split at 11.90. Below it is the deck's underside — the ceiling of the whole main block
# — which is plastered, like the lining walls.clip paints on the inside of its own ring. Above it is
# every outward-facing surface this part has, and all of it is lead: the bays, the rolls, the drips,
# the gutter, its back, and the floors of the four sumps.
#
# 11.90 is the only height that works and it is worth saying why, because the obvious 11.95 is
# wrong. The two numbers it has to separate are the soffit at 11.85 and the FLOOR OF A SUMP at
# 11.91 — the lowest outward-facing surface on this part, 0.09 down inside a gully — and there is
# only 0.06 between them. At 11.95 the sump floors come out plastered: four pale squares at the
# bottom of four bronze gratings, invisible from anywhere and wrong anyway. The wall head is at
# 11.90, which is the natural line for the split and sits 0.05 clear of one and 0.01 clear of the
# other; at the contract's metre 32 the nearest voxel centres are 11.866 below it and 11.926 above,
# so both land on the right side with a voxel to spare.
#
# The gratings are bronze, which is what a gully grating is cast in, and it is the same bronze the
# doors and the entablature's swags are: a second metal beside the lead, in shadow, at 0.045 across.
#
# below=0.02 on every rule and never below=0: the manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm of
# grain at the end, and a voxel that exists only because the grain pushed the surface outward lies
# OUTSIDE the shape its rule names. At below=0 those voxels match nothing, fall back to the base
# coat, and leave a pale limestone rind over a lead roof.
let roof_ceiling = intersection { part_roof   box -16.20 11.50 -7.60   16.20 11.90 7.60 }
let roof_leadwork = intersection { part_roof  box -16.20 11.90 -7.60   16.20 13.10 7.60 }

paint limestone where=part_roof     below=0.02
paint plaster   where=roof_ceiling  below=0.02
paint lead      where=roof_leadwork below=0.02
paint bronze    where=roof_grates   below=0.02
